# Intent: The analog lean of the unit for games (design docs/design/accelerometer-games.md). A pure core (maipai_games_analog_core.h, the page's draft with the packed word added) turns every 20 ms accelerometer sample into a roll and a pitch in permille of a full lean (single pole filter with alpha 0.25, dead zone 0.10 g, full scale 0.50 g about 30 degrees, clamp plus or minus 1000, right and forward positive). It reads nothing (valid false) when the pull is outside 7.0 to 12.6 m/s^2 (carried, shaken) or within 700 ms after a shake. maipai_games_imu_feed runs it on every sample while a game or a grid game is active, using the tilt detector's rest pose, and publishes the result in one atomic 32 bit word; maipai_games_tilt_analog(int* roll_pm, int* pitch_pm) reads it from any task. With no game running the word is 0. The bench tool self.game.bench_tilt {roll_pm, pitch_pm, clear} stands in for the sensor until cleared or the game ends. Safety unchanged: the head never moves, a carried or shaken unit gives no reading. No game uses it yet: Balance, Maze, Racing and Flying come after.
# Placement: unit
# Upstream: firmware/main/hal/maipai_games.cc and maipai_games.h (ours); maipai_games_analog_core.h (new, ours)
# Check after a flash: bench image: start a game; self.game.bench_tilt roll_pm 500 answers valid true and roll_pm 500, clear answers 0, 0; on a still unit on a desk a game's reading is 0, 0; tip the unit gently to the right: roll_pm grows to a positive number; carry or shake it: valid false.
# Kconfig: none (the bench tool is in the bench build only)
# Memory cost: internal RAM 4 bytes for the atomic word and about 40 bytes of filter state (static, in the imu task's file); flash about 0.4 KB for the core and the reader; the bench tool about 0.3 KB in the bench build only.

